 ;; Miscompile at 7f7c556 (62 amu failures) closed by execution, not by
 ;; encodings. Code that executed green is ac14016d; this commit records it.
 ;;
 ;;   8872a54 (before):           1094 tests, 0 failures
 ;;   7f7c556:                    1096 tests, 62 failures
 ;;   7f7c556 + back-edge?:       61 failures
 ;;   ac14016d on judah, amu 30370f07:
 ;;     native-executor-test + isa-execution-test
 ;;     74 tests, 841 assertions, 0 failures
 ;;     string-contains? one-liner returns 1
 ;;
 ;; kotoba-mir and kotoba-native suites still do not execute compiled
 ;; programs. Their green is not this measurement.
 ;;
 ;; Confirmed on the whole suite, not only the two executing namespaces:
 ;; amu 30370f07 is 1096 tests / 8235 assertions / 0 failures across 138 of
 ;; 138 namespaces.
 ;;
 ;; `back-edge?` is NOT load-bearing for that result. Re-run with the
 ;; predicate neutralised, the suite is identical -- 1096 / 8235 / 0 --
 ;; including `let-composes-with-recursion-within-the-fuel-budget`, the one
 ;; failure it had fixed. So `drop-backed-at-label` carries every shape amu
 ;; covers on its own. The neutralisation was proved live rather than assumed:
 ;; the overridden file was made unparseable and the build failed.
 ;;
 ;; The call+loop corpus exists and executes (count-loop, n in {0,1,5,50}
 ;; => n). Neutralising back-edge? is live and the policy stays all-vreg:
 ;; the scanner spill-falls back. A second terminating shape (countdown:
 ;; only n carried, body calls id(n)) does the same. last-uses is textual,
 ;; so a terminating loop's induction variable is defined after the header
 ;; phi that uses it. There is no terminating call+loop the scanner can
 ;; complete while :cfg-liveness is false. Call+loop takes :all-vregs by
 ;; contract. back-edge? stays; remove it when last-uses follow labels.
